menu "Prusa Remote Display network"

    config PRD_WIFI_DRIVER_TIMING
        bool "Record how long each esp_wifi station call takes"
        default n
        help
            Measures all FOUR synchronous station calls the application task now makes —
            esp_wifi_set_config(), esp_wifi_connect(), esp_wifi_disconnect() and
            esp_wifi_sta_get_ap_info() — and emits one timeline record per call carrying the
            duration in microseconds and the esp_err_t the driver returned.

            THE FOURTH IS IN THE SEAM BECAUSE IT MOVED TOO. esp_wifi_sta_get_ap_info() used to
            run on the station's own task; retiring that task put it in observe(), on every
            published observation while Connected. Measuring three of the four would have
            measured three quarters of the change.

            WHY THIS EXISTS. All four are thin wrappers over libnet80211.a blobs, so no
            latency bound for them can be proven by reading the pinned SDK. They now run on
            the application task, which Architecture Specification §10.2 requires to have
            "no long blocking I/O" — so the question is a real acceptance question and the
            only instrument that can answer it is a physical measurement.

            NON-SECRET BY CONSTRUCTION. The timeline signature admits an event name and up
            to two integers and has no way to carry a string value, so no SSID, passphrase,
            API key or address can reach a record. What is emitted is a duration and a
            return code.

            This is diagnostics, not product behaviour. The debug and release profiles leave
            it off. The hardware-validation profile ENABLES it — permanently, by PM decision
            of 2026-08-25 — so that the tree which was reviewed is the tree that gets
            measured: flipping the symbol for the measurement run would have made the
            measured image differ from the reviewed candidate by a source edit.

endmenu
